<p>
I have a copy of SICP, or as it is also known, <i>The Wizard
Book</i>. This book is widely praised, but I can&rsquo;t take the
time to work my way through all of it. Instead, I&rsquo;m going to occasionally jump
into the parts of it that look interesting.
</p>



<p>
In the previous two installations of this series, we looked at ways of
implementing generic functions. In the first one, by tagging values and
dispatching operations on tags. In the second case, by filling a mutable table
of operation–tag pairs. We saw how these are roughly equivalent to the existing
Haskell features of sum types and type classes.
</p>



<p>
Now we&rsquo;ll simulate a digital circuit. The reason this is interesting is that the
solution in SICP uses hidden mutable state and message-passing to make the
code object-oriented. It even uses a mutable global variable for scheduling! I
wasn&rsquo;t sure if it would be possible to replicate that in Haskell, but as we&rsquo;ll
see, it can get incredibly close.
</p>


